WEBVTT

00:00:00.198 --> 00:00:03.160
Here is the one convention that keeps this
tidy.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.673
The derivative of a scalar loss has the
shape of the variable.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:14.610
If W is 512 by 10, then the derivative of
L with respect to W is 512 by 10 as well.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.198
Each entry says how L changes when that
one weight moves.

00:00:18.198 --> 00:00:21.603
And a sum of matching entries of two
matrices is a trace,

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:23.874
the tool that makes the algebra short.

00:00:23.826 --> 00:00:25.838
The recipe has three moves.

00:00:25.838 --> 00:00:29.476
Write the small change in L in terms of
the small change in W.

00:00:29.476 --> 00:00:34.613
Rearrange it into trace of M transpose d
W, using the cyclic rule.

00:00:34.613 --> 00:00:36.701
Then M is the gradient.

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:38.363
There is nothing else to do.

00:00:38.363 --> 00:00:44.304
For example, trace of A transpose W has
the differential trace of A transpose d W,

00:00:44.305 --> 00:00:45.827
so its gradient is A.

00:00:45.752 --> 00:00:47.414
Now a quadratic form:

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:51.577
x transpose A x, with this A, at the point
one, one.

00:00:51.577 --> 00:00:53.764
Many people write the gradient as two A x.

00:00:53.764 --> 00:00:55.052
Is that right here?

00:00:55.052 --> 00:00:58.102
The gradient must cross the contour at a
right angle.

00:00:58.102 --> 00:00:59.702
Pause and decide.

00:01:01.147 --> 00:01:02.172
It is not.

00:01:02.172 --> 00:01:07.122
The product rule gives A plus A transpose,
times x, which is four, eight.

00:01:07.122 --> 00:01:10.509
Write f out by hand and the partial
derivatives agree.

00:01:10.509 --> 00:01:15.259
That arrow crosses the contour at a right
angle, and two A x does not.

00:01:15.259 --> 00:01:18.497
The familiar formula holds only when A is
symmetric.

00:01:18.472 --> 00:01:23.109
A linear layer has inputs X, N by D, and
weights W, D by C.

00:01:23.109 --> 00:01:27.472
The rest of the network only hands it G,
the gradient at its output, N by C.

00:01:27.472 --> 00:01:30.409
We need a D by C matrix, shaped like W.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:35.359
Flip X on its side, and X transpose G is
the only product that fits.

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:39.403
The trace rearrangement gives exactly that
answer, and each entry adds,

00:01:39.404 --> 00:01:43.448
over the batch, an input feature times the
gradient reaching an output.

00:01:43.321 --> 00:01:46.546
A derivation can have the right shape and
still be wrong.

00:01:46.546 --> 00:01:50.758
Two A x and A plus A transpose times x
have the same shape.

00:01:50.758 --> 00:01:54.433
So nudge one entry up and down, and take
the central difference.

00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:57.891
The numbers agree with four, eight to
about ten digits,

00:01:57.892 --> 00:02:01.034
and disagree with two A x in the very
first digit.

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:03.396
Follow the shapes, then trust the check.
